description:
Concurrent code is easiest to test when it does not create threads itself but receives an `Executor`. The program passes a thread pool; a test passes `Runnable::run`, which runs every task at once on the calling thread. Write:

```java
// Parallel.java
class Parallel {
    static <T> List<T> runAll(List<Callable<T>> tasks, Executor executor) throws Exception
    static long sumInChunks(int[] data, int chunks, Executor executor)
}

// SafeCounter.java: a counter that many threads may update at the same time
class SafeCounter {
    void increment()
    void add(long n)
    long get()
    long getAndReset()       // returns the current value and sets it to 0, as one atomic step
}
```

- `runAll` submits every task to the executor (one `execute` call per task, in order) and returns their results in task order. If tasks fail, it throws the exception of the **first failing task in the list** (the original exception, not a wrapper).
- `sumInChunks` splits `data` into `chunks` consecutive parts of nearly equal size (`chunks` from 1 up; never more chunks than items, except that an empty array gives 0), sums each part as its own task on the executor, and adds the part sums. `chunks` below 1 throws `IllegalArgumentException`.
- `SafeCounter` must be correct when used from many threads: use `AtomicLong` (or `LongAdder`, or `synchronized`).

